# Lists

@lede
Two kinds — unordered and ordered. They do not mix at the same level.
Sequential numbering for ordered. Two-space indent for nesting.
@end

## Unordered lists

The marker is `-` followed by exactly one space. `*` and `+` are *not*
valid markers.

```brief
- first item
- second item
- third item
```

That is the only canonical form. Mixing markers, leading spaces, or no
space after the `-` are all errors.

## Ordered lists

The marker is one or more digits, then `.`, then exactly one space.

```brief
1. step one
2. step two
3. step three
```

Numbering must be sequential starting from 1. Out-of-order is @ec[B0501] —
the renderer does *not* silently renumber for you.

```brief
1. one
3. three        // B0501: ordered list numbering must be sequential
```

## Nesting

Nested lists use exactly *two spaces* of indentation per level. One space,
three spaces, or tabs are all errors.

```brief
- top level
  - nested
    - deeper still
  - back to nested
- another top
```

## Mixing kinds across levels

You can switch between ordered and unordered when nesting:

```brief
1. ordered top
  - bullet inside
  - another bullet
2. ordered top again
```

You cannot switch *at the same level*. Switching ends the previous list
and starts a new one.

## Continuation lines

A list item can have continuation lines, indented to match the content
column of the marker (the marker plus its space):

```brief
- this item has
  a continuation line
- next item
```

For ordered items the indent is the digit-width plus the dot plus the
space — usually four spaces for `10.` items, three for `1.` items. The
formatter handles this for you.

## Task lists

Unordered list items can carry a task-state marker as their first content:

```brief
- [x] ship v0.4
- [ ] write release notes
- plain bullet, not a task
```

Rules:

- The marker is exactly `[x] ` (done) or `[ ] ` (todo).
- Lowercase `x`. A single space inside the brackets. A single space after
  the closing bracket.
- Anything else (`[X]`, `[  ]`, `[x]` without the trailing space) is plain
  text.
- Task markers work on ordered items too: `1. [x] foo` renders a checked
  item inside the `<ol>`.

@callout(kind: note)
HTML emit renders these as `<input type="checkbox" disabled>` inside
`<li class="task-list-item">`. The parent `<ul>` carries
`class="contains-task-list"` if any item is a task. LLM emit preserves
the `[x]` / `[ ]` marker verbatim.
@end

## In LLM mode

Indentation drops to one space per nesting level (instead of the source's
two spaces). Markers, numbering, and task states are preserved.
